content.text:
I've applied the fix. Summary:

**Root cause:** the empty active-workspace button had `background: transparent` (so no color showed), but it still carried its own padding (`0 2px`) and default GTK button min-size/border — transparent isn't the same as zero-width, so it still reserved visible horizontal space.

**Fix in `style.css`:** collapsed the whole rule set for taskbar buttons into one that zeroes out size entirely:
```css
#workspaces.taskbar button {
    min-width: 0;
    min-height: 0;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    border: none;
    background: transparent;
}
```
Now a button with no window-icon children has nothing to size itself around, so it collapses to true zero width — not just invisible color. When it *does* have icon children, those are sized by `.taskbar-window`'s own padding/margin (unaffected by this change), so the visible icons look the same as before.

I reloaded waybar and confirmed the non-empty case still renders correctly (divider + 4 icons, no regression). I couldn't safely force a live empty-workspace test myself — your `hyprctl dispatch` is intercepted by some custom Lua layer here that rejects every syntax I tried, and I didn't want to keep poking at a compositor command behaving unpredictably. Could you switch to an empty workspace yourself and confirm it now looks fully clean? If there's still a gap, screenshot it and I'll dig further.

